content.text:
Now main.js. Let me write carefully.

Imports.

```js
import * as THREE from '../vendor/three.module.js';
import { World, BLOCK, BLOCKS, SELL_ORDER, SURFACE, WORLD_X, WORLD_Z, WORLD_Y } from './world.js';
import { Environment } from './environment.js';
import { Player } from './player.js';
import { Particles } from './particles.js';
import { GameAudio } from './audio.js';
import { UI } from './ui.js';
```

Constants:
```
const SAVE_KEY = 'agadh3d.save.v1';
const CENTER = 32;
const SURFACE_SPAWN = {x:32.5,z:42.5};
```

Class Game constructor:
- canvas, renderer, scene, camera
- clock, state etc.
- this.state = 'boot'
- this.pointerLocked=false
- this.isTouch = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches || navigator.maxTouchPoints>0
- this.needSave=false, saveTimer=0
- this.menuT=0
- this.hudTimer=0
- this.jumpQueued=false
- this.keys? player.

Boot method.

World init:
```js
this.saveData = this.loadSave();
this.stateObj = this.saveData ? this.saveData.state : this.defaultState();
this.world = new World(this.scene, this.stateObj.seed);
this.world.generate();
if (this.saveData?.state.world) this.world.decode(this.saveData.state.world);
```
Hmm store world in state; defaultState.world = null. SaveData is just state. Let's use `state.world`.

Load: JSON.parse(localStorage). Validate version and seed.

If save exists, use its state. If state.world decode fails, fresh generated. Fine.

Player spawn:
```
const spawn = this.surfaceSpawn();
this.player = new Player(...); this.player.setSpawn(spawn.x, spawn.y, spawn.z, 0);
```
Need env after world; player after env? Player independent. Environment uses world.

Order: world, env, player, particles, UI.

`this.env.interactables`.

UI binding:
```js
this.ui.bind({
 start: (newGame)=>this.startGame(newGame),
 resume: ()=>this.resumeGame(),
 reset: ()=>this.resetGame(true),
 closeShop: ()=>this.closeShop(),
 sell: ()=>this.sellAll(),
 buy: (id)=>this.buyUpgrade(id),
 mute: ()=>this.toggleMute(),
 keep: ()=>this.keepDigging(),
 again: ()=>this.resetGame(true),
 getGame: ()=>this
});
```

Start screen button "start" passes false (continue) or true (new). If no save, newGame false → start with current default state. Good.

`startGame(newGame)`:
```
this.audio.init();
if (newGame || !this.saveExists) this.resetWorld();
else this.resumeSaved();
this.state='playing'; this.player.enabled=true; this.ui.showGame(); this.ui.updateHUD(this);
this.requestLock();
```
But if no save and default state world generated, resumeSaved just sets position. For no save, use spawn. `resetWorld` regenerates unnecessarily? For first start with no save, we can just reset state and spawn. But if newGame true, regenerate.

Implement:
```js
startGame(isNew) {
 if (isNew) { this.newWorld(); }
 else { this.loadPlayerPosition(); }
 ...
}
```
`newWorld()`: state = defaultState(seed random); this.world.seed = state.seed; this.world.generate(); this.player.setSpawn(...); this.env maybe rebuild? core glow same; ui.last={}; save cleared.
For first-ever start (no save) we already generated with random seed; no need to regenerate. But newWorld would regenerate; acceptable (~0.5s). Could just if state.world null and !isNew, use existing. Let's simplify: on boot if no save, state = defaultState(random seed), world.generate(). On start(isNew=false) just use it (position spawn). If isNew, newWorld().

`loadPlayerPosition`:
```js
const p = this.state.player;
if (p && this.isSafe(p.x,p.y,p.z)) this.player.teleport(...); else spawn;
this.player.yaw = p?.yaw ?? 0;
```
When first start no saved player, spawn.

But after boot with save, player pos could be set immediately; menu camera independent.

`resumeGame` from pause:
```
this.state='playing'; player.enabled=true; ui.hidePause(); requestLock();
```

`pauseGame`:
```
if (this.state!=='playing') return;
this.state='paused'; player.enabled=false; player.digHeld=false; player.keys.clear();
this.ui.showPause({...}); this.saveGame(); // maybe expensive; skip? save lightweight? world encode heavy. Save on pause is okay.
if (document.pointerLockElement) document.exitPointerLock();
```

`resetGame(startNow)`:
```
if (!window.confirm?) handled in UI.
this.newWorld();
if (startNow) { state='playing'; player.enabled=true; ui.hideAll... ui.showGame(); requestLock(); }
else ui.showStart(false);
```
For win "again": resetGame(true).

`keepDigging`: state='playing'; player.enabled=true; ui.hideAllScreens(); ui.showGame(); requestLock().

`toggleMute`: audio.setMuted(!audio.muted); ui.setMuted.

`requestLock`:
```
if (!this.isTouch && document.pointerLockElement !== canvas) {
 try { const p = canvas.requestPointerLock(); if (p?.catch) p.catch(()=>{}); } catch{}
}
```
requestPointerLock returns undefined in older; fine.

`exitLock`: if document.pointerLockElement) document.exitPointerLock().

Input listeners:
- window keydown/up.
- canvas mousedown/up; document mouseup.
- document mousemove.
- pointerlockchange.
- resize.
- contextmenu.
- visibilitychange save.
- pagehide save.

Touch: canvas pointerdown for look if touch. But canvas also receives desktop mouse events; handle separately.

Mobile joystick events with pointer capture. Need avoid canvas look when touching UI.

Implement:
```js
initTouch() {
 const joy = ui.el.joystick;
 let joyId=null;
 joy.addEventListener('pointerdown', e=>{ joyId=e.pointerId; joy.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId); updateJoy(e); });
 joy.addEventListener('pointermove', e=>{ if(e.pointerId===joyId) updateJoy(e); });
 joy.addEventListener('pointerup', ...);
 function updateJoy(e){ const r=joy.getBoundingClientRect(); let dx=e.clientX-(r.left+r.width/2); ... clamp; knob.style.transform=...; player.touchMove.x=dx/42; y=dy/42; }
```
Set knob transform translate.

Dig button pointerdown → digHeld=true; pointerup/cancel → false. Prevent default. Jump → jumpQueued=true. Recall → winch. Interact → interact().

Look drag on canvas: pointerdown if e.pointerType==='touch' and not on UI (canvas receives only canvas touches) → lookId; lastX/Y; pointermove → player.look(dx,dy) with multiplier 1.6. pointerup reset. This conflicts with dig button? no, separate elements.

Desktop mousedown: if e.button===0 and state playing → digHeld=true; button 2 → placeQueued=true. Only when pointerLocked? If not locked, clicking might be a UI? Canvas click when playing but not locked could request lock. Use:
```
if (state==='playing') {
 if (!pointerLocked && !isTouch) this.requestLock();
 if (e.button===0) player.digHeld=true;
 ...
}
```
But after clicking start, pointer lock requested; fine. On pause overlay, canvas isn't clicked (overlay covers). Shop overlay covers.

Prevent context menu.

Mouse look:
```
if (state==='playing' && (pointerLocked || this.lookDragging)) player.look(e.movementX||0, e.movementY||0)
```
When not locked, movementX still provided but only if dragging? Use pointerLocked only. Fine.

Interact:
```
